WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.680
Did people curse in the real&nbsp;
Deadwood like on HBO's "Deadwood"?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.080
How did the movie get made 13 years&nbsp;
after the show ended? How did the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.920
actors handle constant rewrites? Keep&nbsp;
watching for all the dirty details.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:22.560
David Milch, the maestro behind "Deadwood,"&nbsp;
came to HBO well into a storied career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:29.600
He began writing for "Hill Street Blues" in 1982,&nbsp;
beginning a two-decade run of bringing his dense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.560
literary style to crime and legal&nbsp;
dramas. His most successful endeavor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.920
to that point was co-creating the&nbsp;
show "NYPD Blue" with Steven Bochco.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.880
By the early 2000s, he was ready to&nbsp;
create a new show using law enforcement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.600
as a window into the way a community is&nbsp;
formed. But the original inspiration was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:56.080
not the time or place you'd think. In a 2006&nbsp;
interview with American Heritage, Milch said,

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:01.520
"My initial feeling was that I'd set it in&nbsp;
ancient Rome, the time of the emperor Nero."

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.800
But HBO already had the series "Rome" in&nbsp;
production, which they felt covered too much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.240
the same ground. So they asked Milch if he could&nbsp;
explore the same themes of order forming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:18.400
chaos, in a different historical setting. Milch&nbsp;
realized that the city of Deadwood, which circa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:25.840
1876 was a lawless mining camp in Native American&nbsp;
territory, was exactly the setting he needed.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:31.200
One of the most striking things about&nbsp;
"Deadwood" is its unabashed vulgarity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:37.280
Milch didn't specifically set out to dispel&nbsp;
the genteel myths of American Western movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.040
but HBO gave him full freedom&nbsp;
to use as much violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:47.440
nudity, and foul language as he needed to depict&nbsp;
his vision of the West as accurately as possible.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.920
"Welcome to f---ing Deadwood."

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.400
The language, specifically, became the&nbsp;
most talked-about aspect of "Deadwood"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.520
when it premiered, causing most viewers to&nbsp;
immediately wonder: did they really curse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.920
like that in the real Deadwood? Speaking with NPR,&nbsp;
the curator of Deadwood's Adams Museum admitted,

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.640
"The short answer is yes, Deadwood&nbsp;
was a very profane place."

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.960
There is ample evidence that all of the&nbsp;
curse words used on "Deadwood" existed at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:21.520
the time the show is set. Scholar and dictionary&nbsp;
editor Jesse Sheidlower told New York Magazine:

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.320
"The evidence that we have&nbsp;
is that they were using more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:28.800
religious blasphemy than the sexual&nbsp;
insults which are popular today."

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:34.400
But instead of using antiquated terms like&nbsp;
"gol-darn" and "tarnation," Milch and co.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.840
went with language that would be as shocking&nbsp;
to modern audiences as the citizenry intended.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.320
If you're a fan of "Deadwood," you might think&nbsp;
the first role cast was one of the more central,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.400
prominent characters on the show. Who will deliver&nbsp;
the crudely eloquent monologues of Al Swearengen?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:58.800
Who will embody the simmering rage of Seth&nbsp;
Bullock, or the anxious gentility of Alma Garret?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:04.800
But a chance encounter in 2002 led to a&nbsp;
small role being the first official casting:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.280
Jewel, the sweet, disabled&nbsp;
cleaning woman at the Gem Saloon.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.560
As actress Geri Jewell told Yahoo! Entertainment,

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:17.200
"I was at Horton &amp; Converse drugstore&nbsp;
in Santa Monica, standing in line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.680
This man turned around, and he said,&nbsp;
'Oh, my God. Are you Geri Jewell?'"

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.440
It was none other than future&nbsp;
"Deadwood" creator David Milch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:31.040
who recognized Jewell as Cousin Geri on "The&nbsp;
Facts of Life," a role which broke ground as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:37.200
first recurring role for an actor with cerebral&nbsp;
palsy. He promptly asked her to be on the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.320
and after one more meeting, the first&nbsp;
member of his sprawling ensemble was cast.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:49.520
Despite a main cast of well over a dozen, and&nbsp;
hundreds of recurring guest stars, bit parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:56.240
and extras, no actor boosted their reputation more&nbsp;
during the course of "Deadwood" than Ian McShane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:02.960
As Al Swearengen, the owner/operator of the Gem&nbsp;
Saloon, McShane was central to the labyrinthian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:09.280
plot of the show. But surprisingly, the role&nbsp;
wasn't originally written with McShane in mind.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.720
David Milch wrote the role for Ed O'Neill,&nbsp;
best known as Al Bundy on "Married... with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.120
Children." According to O'Neill in&nbsp;
an interview with Erik Hedegaard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.360
David Milch was so certain on casting&nbsp;
decisions that he assured him,

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.520
"You're going to be my Swearengen."

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:31.200
Milch was convinced that O'Neill had the dramatic&nbsp;
chops necessary to overcome his reputation as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:37.680
a recognizable comedy star. Unfortunately, HBO&nbsp;
hadn't been consulted at all on the decision at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.280
that point. The network wanted to cast someone&nbsp;
as relatively unknown as the rest of the cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.520
and suggested McShane. As Milch told C&amp;I Magazine,

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.800
"The minute [McShane] walked in, he just took&nbsp;
ownership of the role and my imagination."

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.720
The most recognizable "Deadwood" cast&nbsp;
member is almost certainly Keith Carradine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:04.480
Known for dozens of Western roles, he was so&nbsp;
well-prepared to play Wild Bill in "Deadwood"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:10.320
that he brought his own wig, custom made for him&nbsp;
during production of Walter Hill's "Wild Bill."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.600
Speaking with The AV Club, Carradine&nbsp;
says he received a call from Walter Hill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.400
now set to direct the pilot of "Deadwood,"&nbsp;
who asked him what his hair looked like.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:24.640
"I said, 'Well, it's short, Walter,&nbsp;
but... I've still got that wig!'"

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.880
Reception of his performance was so&nbsp;
positive that HBO initially pushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.880
back on the character's planned death&nbsp;
in the fourth episode of the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.040
matching the timeline of the real Wild&nbsp;
Bill's infamous shooting during a hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:42.720
of poker. Carradine recalled his conversation&nbsp;
with Milch at the time to Yahoo! Entertainment:

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.160
"[Milch] said, 'the good news&nbsp;
is they love the show […] the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.960
bad news is they don't want me to kill Hickok.'"

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.400
Despite the network's note getting his hopes up&nbsp;
for a longer run, Milch won the battle and stuck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:06:00.160
to the original plan. The character's presence,&nbsp;
aided by Carradine's pitch-perfect performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.960
reverberated throughout&nbsp;
the rest of the show's run.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.200
Praised for its dialogue, plotting, and depth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.800
the success of "Deadwood" begins&nbsp;
and ends with the writing.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:17.120
"Could you have been born, Richardson, and&nbsp;
not egg-hatched as I've always assumed?"

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:20.960
And while there was a large&nbsp;
team of writers on the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:25.600
nearly every script was heavily&nbsp;
re-written by showrunner David Milch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:31.040
to better fit the rhythm of the language he&nbsp;
heard in his mind — sometimes on the spot.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:36.240
It wasn't just the writers that had to swallow&nbsp;
their pride — actors needed to adjust to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:42.640
demanding task of learning several new pages&nbsp;
of dialogue on the spot. Actor Brad Dourif,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:48.400
who played fan-favorite Doc Cochran,&nbsp;
recalled a particularly infuriating incident:

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.480
"One time he came up to me, and said,&nbsp;
'You see this line?' And he gave me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.920
four pages. 'I'm replacing this line with&nbsp;
this,' and it was a four-page monologue."

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:04.480
Well before "Game of Thrones" gained notoriety&nbsp;
for killing off main characters, "Deadwood" — by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:09.840
the virtue of its setting and commitment to&nbsp;
authenticity — was the best example of a show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:15.600
willing to kill off major characters without&nbsp;
warning. It was simply a reality of the West,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:21.040
and the particular power struggles that came&nbsp;
with being a lawless, frontier mining camp.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:26.800
Viewers unfamiliar with history were likely&nbsp;
shocked when Wild Bill Hickok was shot and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:32.640
killed in just the fourth episode of Season 1.&nbsp;
Plenty of characters would succumb to illness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:38.320
stabbings, gunfights and more over "Deadwood's"&nbsp;
three seasons and feature-length movie.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:40.480
"Run for your f---ing life."

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:46.640
Speaking with Inside Pulse during the filming&nbsp;
of Season 2, Jim Beaver shared the anxiety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:52.400
that he felt over the potential fate of his&nbsp;
character, pure-hearted prospector Ellsworth:

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.320
"Everybody on 'Deadwood' is worried&nbsp;
they're going to end up pig chow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:01.120
Well, maybe not everybody, but I think&nbsp;
the worriers outnumber the non-worriers!"

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:04.652
Ellsworth was killed by an&nbsp;
assassin at the end of Season 3.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:11.440
One of the most memorable moments in all of&nbsp;
"Deadwood" is the Season 3 fight between Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:18.640
Dority, played by W. Earl Brown, and villainous&nbsp;
henchman Captain Turner. Milch wanted the fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:25.280
to be completely realistic and avoid Western&nbsp;
clichés, or as Brown recounted to Slashfilm:

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.520
"No cowboys flying through plate glass."

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:32.160
So the actors and crew found&nbsp;
themselves coordinating a bare-hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:38.080
all-out brawl in the mud of the thoroughfare&nbsp;
with no clue how to end it. Fate intervened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:43.440
at a poker game Brown was playing with&nbsp;
Alice in Chains guitarist Jerry Cantrell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:48.480
Apparently, his brother Dave Cantrell had&nbsp;
once been badly losing a bar fight, when:

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:52.720
"In desperation, he tried to push the&nbsp;
guy off of him. He felt his finger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:57.040
hit soft tissue and knew without&nbsp;
seeing it, that it was an eyeball."

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:00.640
And so the makers of "Deadwood"&nbsp;
found their way to have Dority,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:06.800
seemingly about to lose his life, turn the tables&nbsp;
by clawing out Turner's eye in a stroke of luck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:10.800
and by far the most intense&nbsp;
visual of the show was born.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:18.720
Beyond well-known Old West heroes like Wild Bill&nbsp;
and Calamity Jane, many characters in "Deadwood"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:24.800
were based on real citizens of the historical&nbsp;
city of Deadwood. Central characters like Al&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:31.440
Swearengen, Sol Starr, and Seth Bullock were&nbsp;
based on real people, although many characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:37.360
had slightly different fates than their real-life&nbsp;
counterparts. The show’s legacy has driven tourism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:43.280
to the real Deadwood to such a degree that there&nbsp;
are many specific travel packages designed around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:50.160
the show and movie. Visitors can experience the&nbsp;
"Deadwood: Heroes &amp; Villains" tour, among others.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:54.000
You can even get a guided walking&nbsp;
tour from the town's first Marshall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.440
or at least a re-enactor playing&nbsp;
the part of Con Stapleton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:02.800
who was also a character in "Deadwood." While&nbsp;
they welcome visitors, the city's official website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:08.960
does have an entire section of its website about&nbsp;
differences between the HBO show and real life.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:12.160
In interviews, time and time again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:16.640
the cast recall their time on "Deadwood"&nbsp;
as a head above all the rest. Speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:22.000
with the AV Club, W. Earl Brown recalled&nbsp;
something a fellow actor once told him:

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:24.720
"He said 'I don't really care about money, fame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:29.360
and that stuff. What I want to do is something&nbsp;
that has an impact.' [...] And 'Deadwood' was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:35.040
that for me [...] I think it was, for everyone&nbsp;
involved, something really, really special."

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:40.640
Keith Carradine remarked to the AV Club as well&nbsp;
that it wasn't just the role of Wild Bill Hickok&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:46.320
that attracted him to the part, but the mad genius&nbsp;
behind "Deadwood" that made it more memorable:

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:51.280
"I mean, what a role to have the chance to&nbsp;
play, but especially as written by Milch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:56.240
It was extraordinary stuff. He wrote&nbsp;
this kind of American Shakespeare."

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:01.120
Reflecting on returning to her role as&nbsp;
Calamity Jane for "Deadwood: The Movie,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:03.440
Robin Weigert told Slashfilm,

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:08.240
"We were getting incredible words to say.&nbsp;
And I knew that I was with an exceptional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:13.840
cast – that everybody I got to partner&nbsp;
with in scenes was humbling, wonderful."

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:20.960
After a slow-motion death in 2006 — when HBO and&nbsp;
David Milch debated about production schedules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:25.600
costs, and the length of a potential&nbsp;
Season 4 for so long that the casts'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:32.320
contracts expired — there were rumors of everyone&nbsp;
reuniting for one or possibly two wrap-up movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:38.480
But each tii me the plans didn't come to fruition,&nbsp;
it sank the sprawling cast's spirits further until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:44.640
it seemed all hope was lost. Asked by Vulture if&nbsp;
he ever thought the movie would really happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:49.040
the famously verbose Ian McShane&nbsp;
replied with a curt "No."

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:54.000
Even W. Earl Brown, who for years&nbsp;
maintained a hand-written list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.480
every main cast member's commitments&nbsp;
for several years just in case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:03.040
lost the faith at some point.&nbsp;
In 2019 he told Slashfilm,

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:06.640
"I gave up beating the dead&nbsp;
horse after a few years."

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:11.440
Finally, on the strength of "Deadwood's"&nbsp;
strong DVD sales and streaming legacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:17.360
Milch and HBO struck a deal. "Deadwood:&nbsp;
The Movie" premiered on HBO in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:20.800
garnering high praise from both critics and fans.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:23.840
"What a grand surprise&nbsp;
after such a piece of time."

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:30.720
In 2015, David Milch received a diagnosis&nbsp;
of Alzheimer's. Just months later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:35.680
he finally reached an agreement with HBO&nbsp;
to write a script for "Deadwood: The Movie"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:41.840
and began a four year process of wrapping up&nbsp;
the story. His usual mad rush of re-assembling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:48.000
sets and scenes on a whim and last-minute rewrites&nbsp;
were tempered by the symptoms of the disease. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:54.160
relied more on the director and other producers&nbsp;
to run things on set, and stuck to the script.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:58.640
But that script was still pure Milch&nbsp;
magic, and writing has proved to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:04.080
Milch's best weapon to hold on to his mind&nbsp;
in the face of the disease's progression.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:10.240
Speaking with Vulture, his wife Rita described&nbsp;
it with an analogy perfect for the legendary TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:15.280
creator, who wrote an ensemble cast's&nbsp;
voices like a symphony conductor:

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:20.880
"I compare it to a musician who can still play&nbsp;
and has access to the memory of how to do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:26.160
and is still able to exercise his talent. [...]&nbsp;
The brain is David's most exercised muscle."

